were  [Music]  the ukrainian women and children akshank  sharma shows videos of him and his  fellow students fleeing the russian  bombing of kharkiv the 20 year old was  in his first year of medical school in  the eastern ukrainian city he's one of  nearly 18 000 indian medical students  evacuated from ukraine since the russian  invasion began  last year  i passed the entrance exam last year but  i didn't have enough marks to go to  government college here because their  cut-off requirements are very high so  the better option for me was to go  overseas  like akshayang sisters monica and anshul  bhatti said they were forced to study  overseas where fees are much cheaper  their family says they're paying half  the amount of fees in ukraine than they  would in private colleges in india  online classes have begun for the  sisters and some others but they're not  ideally  [Music]  the problem with online is that we need  a lot of practical experience which we  aren't able to do we are a third year  student and practical is very important  in the third year so it's difficult  the return of the students from ukraine  is again putting the spotlight on the  lack of medical colleges in india  the number of seats available for  medical education in india is far less  than the number of students who leave  school with the hope of becoming doctors  of the 1.6 million who did the entrance  exam last year only 88 000 made it into  the 562  medical colleges in the country  given the shortage private colleges  charge hefty fees for the much  sought-after spaces and receive large  donations from students families  public health experts blame a lack of  government investment in health care and  what they call the medical council of  india's arbitrary regulations to set up  colleges  whether it was a bonafide  intent to protect standards as they said  or whether it was to protect some  interests of people who are running  private medical colleges i cannot really  say  however  that did result in a smaller number of  medical colleges and also a clustering  of medical colleges  in the four southern states and two  western indian states  prime minister narendra modi blamed  previous governments for the lack of  colleges and said his administration  will increase the number  as students who've returned from ukraine  appealed to the government to find a way  for them to continue their studies at  home  some health experts say it's an ideal  opportunity for india to tackle the  doctor shortage elizabeth paranam al  jazeera greater noida uttar pradesh

